% c200 (release check): brass dynamics at 333-339 as they now stand, superseding the brass levels named in the older c028, c034 and c128 comments below: horns ff from 333 through 339 (c199), Tpt 1-2 f (c194), trombones, bass trombone and tuba f (c169); no brass plays fff, ff on an attack, sfz or accents (Dan: weight, not bite). The single fff at 336 is in strings, woodwinds and timpani.
% c186: 339 "non div." on the struck multiple stops of Vln II (four notes), violas (three) and cellos (two), as at 72, 105, 300 and 305: c025 wants every note sounded by every player, and a tutti section divides an unmarked chord by habit (Adler PDF 30: "it is safer to write non div."; PDF 31-32, Ex. II-12: the division of triple and quadruple stops is to be specified).
% c175: 358 Vc and Cb up-bow: with the bow alternating from 358 the held E 361-364 fell on an up-bow under its diminuendo; starting up-bow puts the four-bar diminuendo on a down-bow, as in Vln II and violas (Adler PDF 38: diminuendo tends to the down-bow; mark the bow only to counteract habit).
% c175: 352 violas "unis." on the single E4 inside the div. passage 349-356, "div." again at 354 (Adler PDF 30: when divisi is no longer in order and the part returns to single notes, unis. should appear).
% c128: 339 brass (Hn 1-4, Tpt 1-2, Tbn 1-2, B.tbn, tuba) hold a tenuto minim with a diminuendo under the strings', woodwinds' and timpani's short accented stroke
%   (was an accented crotchet, low brass entering fff): the stroke comes from the short notes, the weight from the long ones (Adler II PDF 189-190, Ex. XV-35;
%   PDF 39-40, Tchaikovsky 4/i: the climactic brass hold fff> notes); Dan's rule (BRIEF: brass weight, not bite; no accented brass entries). Low brass ff, a degree under
%   the horns' fff, as c086 sets them at 333.
% c128: 335 Tpt 1-2 crescendo into 336 with the rest of the orchestra (they jumped f -> fff); 336-338 tenuto, not accented (c124's rule for the tutti theme).
% c104: 339 Fl 2 B5 (was E6 in unison with Fl 1): the fifth of the last ff chord in the woodwind above the trumpets, which had only E and G there since c044 moved Cl 2 to G4 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 110-111; Dvorak 9/iii last stroke: Ob 1 and Cl 1 on B).
% c082: 337 Hn 1 B4-C5-B4 (336-338): the flat 9th as upper neighbour within V, 9-8 without change of chord (A-S PDF 464-466, Ex. 27-4a, 27-5); C5 resolves to B4 at 338, the 7th A stays in Tpt 1.
% c154: 336-338 Hn 3 F sharp 4 in unison with Hn 2 (was D sharp 4 with Tpt 2): above mf two horns in unison equal one trumpet or trombone (Adler II PDF 15,
%   Ex. XI-1; PDF 44, Bartok), so the fifth of V no longer rests on one horn while the leading tone had trumpet and horn; D sharp stays in Tpt 2, Cl 1, Fl 2.
%   339 Hn 1 holds B4 (was G4): the brass chord's fifth was in Hn 4 alone at ff; G stays in Tpt 1 and Tbn 1 (Adler II PDF 15-16, Ex. XI-1: complete triads in the choir).
% CODA, bars 325-376 (c030: header renumbered from the v03 numbers 333-384; the finale thread reads these bar numbers).
% c041: 345-348 T's second phrase (A major, D5-B5) in Ob 1, dolce, not Cl 1: concert B5 is the A clarinet's written D6, where Adler (I PDF 210, Ex. VII-56)
%   puts the 'piercing, shrill' register, the only soft clarinet note above written C6 in the movement; F4-C6 is the oboe's 'warm, prominent, reedy and
%   poignant' range and the oboe is 'basically a lyrical instrument' (PDF 198). The oboe had no phrase of T; Dvorak 9/iii gives its E-major tune to Ob 1 (68-75).
% c035: 361 Fl 1 prints "1." (no label was reprinted after the shared rest).
% c022: iv6 at 331 (C-E-A over C, Phrygian approach to V; c022 called it 339 in v23 numbers) replaces the diminished triad C sharp-E-G (A-S PDF 167 Ex. 11-6c, 168): Vln I C-A, Cl 2, Bsn 2, Cb, Cbsn on C, Bsn 1 A; no G, so no C-major triad.
% 325-340 Più mosso: motive x as a stretto ostinato entering from the bottom (Vc 325, Vla 326, Vln II 327, Vln I 328), pp crescendo; the bass falls
%   E-D-C-B (329-332); theme A's head ff (333); Vln I and Fl 1 peak on B6 at 336, fff, the movement's only fff (the scherzo's highest note, a semitone under the finale's C7);
%   hemiola 337-338, E minor chord (339), general pause (340).
% 341-376 Meno mosso (tempo del Trio), T returns p in Hn 1 (341, A major), Ob 1 (345; c041), then in E Vln I with Vln II and Cl 1 an octave lower (349; c044: no Fl 1), cadence 353-356 (c043).
% c103: header line above brought up to date (it still named Cl 1 at 345 and Fl 1 at 349; Aldwell-Schachter PDF 450: a plan is only meaningful when checked against the score).
%   c013: T's first half (341-348) is in A major, the tonicized IV that a late coda can carry (Aldwell-Schachter Unit 26 sec. 11, PDF 451-452);
%   its half cadence on E (348, V of A) is the pivot back to E (I), and T's second half (349-356) confirms E with ii-ii6-6/4-V-I.
% 357-360: Hn 1 sings the motto's chromatic tail on the fifth degree, B-A sharp-A-G sharp (E flat-D-D flat-C transposed),
%   over E - A sharp dim7 - A minor (iv, mixture) - E: a plagal close that ends on G sharp.
% 361-376: E major held pp, then only Hn 1 and Vln I on G sharp (the pitch that opened the scherzo, movement II's A flat) over the timpani's motive x (363, 367, 371; c090)
%   and a last pizzicato E with the harp's harmonics (375); the finale takes that G sharp as A flat (Hn 1 A flat 4, Vln I A flat 5).

% c043: 353-356 the movement's last authentic cadence is expanded: the cadential 6/4 holds two bars (353-354; Vln I/Fl 1 G sharp 6-F sharp-E over B,
%   the 6-5-4 descent of the 6/4's upper voice, theme A's minim-two-quaver rhythm), V7 at 355, I at 356, so it is no longer a copy of the trio tune's
%   internal cadences (165-167, 181-183, 221-223). Aldwell-Schachter Unit 19 PDF 317-318 (expanding the cadential 6/4; Ex. 19-21, 19-22 Brahms at the end
%   of the Requiem), PDF 314-315 (6-5-4-3 above a held bass), PDF 307 (the accented 6/4 delays the expected arrival). No bar added. Violas 354 G sharp-E (6/4),
%   355 A-F sharp (V7; the inner 4th rises, Vln I's falls: PDF 317). Vc/Cb pizz. B 353 and 355, E 356; arco from 358 (Bsn 2 holds E at 357), so pizz.->arco keeps 4 beats.
% c042: 358-360 violas G4-E4-E4 (were G4-A4-G sharp 4): the augmented 2nd G-A sharp against Hn 1 opens to the 4th E-A instead of closing into
%   a unison on Hn 1's A4, so the motto tail's A and G sharp sound alone at their pitch (A-S PDF 375 Ex. 22-2, 377); harmony and Hn 1 unchanged.
% c397: 325-328 the stretto continues each entry with theme A's chromatic tail, as the fugato's stretto at 69-72 does, instead of four voices repeating motive x in one rhythm
%   (parallel fifths and octaves at 326-328, the entries heard as doublings). Each entry is x; the voice that entered the bar before goes on with the tail
%   (Vc G3-F#3-F3 326, Vla D4-C#4-C4 on B 327, Vln II G5-F#5-F5 328), each tail resolving into the next downbeat. The upper entries take x's repeated-note form
%   (B3, E5, B5; the timpani's B-B-B-B of 89-100 and 241-252) so no entry drops under the held tail note. Adler II PDF 44-47 (Bartok, Concerto for Orchestra i, 363-386:
%   stretto entries a bar apart, each voice continuing the subject in its own rhythm); PDF 42 (Hindemith: every entry comes through clearly). Order, pitches of entry, pp and crescendo unchanged; 329-339 unchanged.
% c028: 325-328 the ostinato entries stay pp until Vln I has entered (328); all strings cresc. from 328, mf at 329, ff at 333 (Adler II PDF 37: hold back for the event).
% c028: 336 fff in all parts: Vln I's B6 and the timpani roll are the movement's peak, above the ff of 72, 104-120, 300-320 and 333 (Adler II PDF 38-40, Tchaikovsky 4/i).
% c032: 329-332 Vln I in 10ths above the falling bass (G-F#-E-D#, with Cl 1 an octave lower), not in octaves with it; the E-B drone gives way
%   to chord tones at 331 (iv6: A-E) and 332 (V: B-F#) in Vc, Vla, Vln II, so no B sounds against the bass C before the bass reaches B (A-S Unit 17, PDF 267-269, 277-279).
% c034: 336-338 trombones, bass trombone and tuba rest under the B6 peak and re-enter fff on the tonic chord at 339 (Adler II PDF 150, Barber Sym. 1:
%   trombones and tuba left out of the bar of the high note so it is not made heavy; the low brass entrance on the last chord gives the finality).
% c034: 333-335 Cl 1 (E5) and the violas (E4) take theme A's head with Vln I/Fl 1 and Vln II/Ob 1 instead of sustaining E: the agile instruments
%   carry the figure in octaves while the brass sustain (Adler II PDF 154, Smetana; PDF 150-151, the octave tutti that summarises an idea).
% c036: Fl 1 p, not pp: its line climbs to A6, the flute's top fourth, where soft dynamics and intonation are hardest to control (Adler PDF 174, 186)
% c044: 349-356 Fl 1 rests: the soft last statement of T is Vln I alone on top (pp, up to A6 and G sharp 6) with Cl 1 doubling Vln II's lower octave, not Fl 1 p over
%   Vln I pp at pitch. Adler II PDF 162 (Brahms 3/i last chord: a soft high flute overpowers; easier with the strings on top and the winds an octave below),
%   PDF 164 (violins play softly on any note), PDF 165 (doublings at pitch lose both colours), PDF 170-171 (a recurring passage coloured differently each time).
% c044: 361 Fl 1's manual "1." removed: after the 20-bar rest the part combiner prints the label itself (it printed twice).
% c044: 339 Cl 2 G4 (was B4): its seventh A4 (336-338) falls to G with Tpt 1's (Adler II PDF 161-162, Schumann 1/i: all sevenths resolve downward).
% c213: 330 Cbsn enters on D2 under the bass clarinet's last note (was 331 mp), so the sustained wind bass of the descent E-D-C-B passes from B.cl. to Cbsn on a common note (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 125-126) and the Cbsn enters p, not mp (PDF 136; PDF 127: the second group enters p and crescendos faster)
% c050: 329 the woodwinds (Cl 1-2, B.cl, Bsn 1-2) enter p, not mf at the strings' level, and crescendo faster to the ff at 333; Cbsn p at 331
%   (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 127, amplification: while the first group makes the crescendo gradually, the second enters piano and reaches its crescendo more rapidly).
% c050: 357-360 Vln II G sharp 4-G 4-E 4-E 4 and violas (with Bsn 1) E4/G sharp 3-C sharp 4-C 4-B 3: each chromatic step of the plagal close (G sharp-G, C sharp-C) is made in one voice,
%   so Vln II's G sharp 4 no longer meets the violas' G4 in the next chord (false relation), and Bsn 1 no longer doubles Hn 1's A-G sharp in octaves (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 126-127).
%   Hn 1's motto tail and the harmony E - A sharp dim7 - iv6/4 - E are unchanged; 361-364 Vln II G sharp 4, violas B3 (exchanged with the old parts).
% c061: 354-355 the quaver E of the 6-5-4 descent is tied into the V7 bar and resolves to D sharp on beat 2: the trio's new V4-3 (tA.ily) at the end of
%   the expanded 6/4 (Aldwell-Schachter PDF 353-354 Ex. 21-11b, 6/4 into 4-3 as a cadence formula; PDF 361 sec. 21, a delayed resolution builds tension). Violas A-F sharp, no D sharp on beat 1.
% c098: 355 > from the tied E (the delayed 4-3 at the end of the 6-5-4 descent) to D sharp (A-S PDF 348, 359 sec. 21). 357-359 Hn 1 < into A sharp, then > through A to G sharp and the pp at 361: A sharp (in the A sharp dim7) and A (the 4th of the iv6/4) are dissonant against the tonic pedal E, G sharp is their resolution (A-S PDF 348, 350 sec. 3, 368-370 sec. 28). Notes unchanged.
% c166: 335 Vln I and Fl 1 climb E6-F#6-G6-A6 into B6 in motive x's rhythm (were E6-B5-B5-G6 in Vln I and E6-B5-B5-G6-A6 in Fl 1): Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 26,
%   notes above the violins' practical limit only in long values or 'not too rapid sequence of scales'; 'skips should always be avoided'. Removes the leaps B5-G6-B6
%   and the G6/A6 rub between the two top parts on beat 3. Vln II, violas, Cl 1, Ob 2 and trumpets keep E-B-B-G.
% c126: 335 Fl 1 G6-A6 quavers into the B6 at 336 (was G6 crotchet, a leap of a third onto B6): Adler I PDF 186, above A6 the notes are hard to produce and the best approach is by scale. Vln I keeps motive x.
codaFluteOne = { R2.*8 | e'''4-.\ff b''8-. b''-. e'''4-. | g'''2->( fis'''8 f'''8 | e'''4-.)\< fis'''8-. g'''-. a'''4( | b'''2.->\fff) | a'''2-> fis'''4->~ | fis'''4 dis'''2-> | e'''4-> r4 r4 | R2. | R2.*8 | R2.*8 | R2.*4 | gis''2.~\pp | gis''2.~\> | gis''2.~ | gis''2 r4\! | R2.*12 | }
% c029: 333-339 Fl 2 and Ob 2 fill the chord above the trumpets (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 110-111): at 336 B6-D#6-B5-F#5-D#5 over Tpt B4-D#4 (was B-B-B-B).
% c288: 336-338 the held woodwind notes of the peak chord (Fl 2 D#6, Cl 1 D#5, Cl 2 A4, Ob 2 F#5) slurred with tenuto, as c188 did for the horns in the same bars:
%   unmarked, they were re-tongued on 338's downbeat, where the hemiola minim of Fl 1, Ob 1, C.a., bassoons and strings is tied over (Adler I PDF 309, 321). fff and pitches unchanged.
codaFluteTwo = { R2.*8 | b''2.\ff | b''2. | b''2.\< | dis'''2.--(\fff | dis'''2.-- | dis'''2.--) | b''4-> r4 r4 | R2. | R2.*36 | }
% s125 (cycle 125): 333-339 the oboes no longer cross: Ob 2 held G5 above Ob 1's E5-B4 figure, so the two parts' noteheads overlapped on one staff
%   (Gould, Behind Bars PDF 65-67: upper part up-stems, parts kept clearly apart). Ob 1 now holds the upper line, Ob 2 plays the figure; the sound is unchanged.
codaOboeOne = { R2.*8 | g''2.\ff | g''2->( fis''8 f''8) | g''2.\< | b''2.->\fff | a''2-> fis''4->~ | fis''2. | g''4-> r4 r4 | R2. | R2.*4 | d''2(\p^\markup \italic "dolce" e''4 | fis''4\< b''2-\tweak stencil ##f \mp\> | a''4.-\tweak stencil ##f \p gis''8 fis''4 | dis''4 e''2) | R2.*28 | }
% c291: 335 Vln II, C.a. and violas climb E-F#-G-A into the peak with Vln I and Fl 1 (were E-B-B-G, motive x): theme A's rise F#-G-A-B to its peak
%   is in every octave at 115/315; here the same rise to the movement's only fff and B6 was in the top octave alone over the old figure, the A6 against G5/G4 below
%   (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 68-69: a melody high in the compass is doubled an octave lower, in octaves keeping the natural order; PDF 77 Vla+C.a.). Ob 2, Cl 1 and the trumpets keep motive x (Ob 2 would cross above Ob 1's held G5).
codaOboeTwo = { R2.*8 | e''4-.\ff b'8-. b'-. e''4-. | g''2->( fis''8 f''8 | e''4-.)\< b'8-. b'-. g''4-. | fis''2.--(\fff | fis''2.-- | fis''4--) dis''2-> | e''4-> r4 r4 | R2. | R2.*36 | }
% c087: 333-339 the cor anglais doubles the violas' lower octave of theme A's head and the falling line (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 79-80: Violas + Eng. horn + Horn, the three groups in unison,
%   used for preference in loud passages, the wood-wind giving a fuller and more evenly blended tone; PDF 76: the wind increases the string's resonance;
%   Ex. 87, Kashtchei, PDF 261: in a ff melody in octaves over held brass the lowest octave carries violas, cellos, 2 Cl, Eng. horn and Fag.). The violas' E4 sat alone under four ff horns (c084);
%   the woodwinds now have the head in three octaves, as the strings do (Fl 1 E6, Ob 1 and Cl 1 E5, C.a. E4), and the C.a. returns as in the tuttis at 105 and 305 (c021).
codaCorAnglais = { R2.*8 | e'4-.\ff b8-. b-. e'4-. | g'2->( fis'8 f'8 | e'4-.)\< fis'8-. g'-. a'4-. | b'2.->\fff | a'2-> fis'4->~ | fis'4 dis'2-> | e'4-> r4 r4 | R2. | R2.*36 | }
% c250: the scherzo is the symphony's only movement with clarinets in A (I, II and IV use B flat); the change back is marked at the end, as the
%   timpani's retuning for the Finale is (c020), so the players take the B flat clarinets in the pause (MOLA PDF 5: "clear indications of when to change
%   followed by a statement of which instrument is being played"; Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 21, axiom II: a work stands the best chance when the parts are well written).
%   Printed once on the shared score staff (Cl 1), in both parts. Movement II is asked to mark "muta in A" at its end.
% c268: 376 the score's muta text gets a white box (whiteout, layer 3) so the woodwind span bar at 375|376 no longer runs through "muta in B flat" and "Finale" (MOLA PDF 8 and Ex. 7, PDF 12: anchored markings must not collide).
% c255: in the parts the muta goes below the stave of the last bar (\tag #'part). Above it, on the ragged last page, it was stacked over the fermata
%   and sat nearer the system above (Cl 1: under bar 354, while Cl 1 still plays the A clarinet at 361-364) than its own bar (MOLA PDF 8, Ex. 7 PDF 12:
%   extracted parts leave anchored markings floating). The score keeps it above the staff (\tag #'score; score.ly strips 'part from Cl 1).
% c379: 349-356 Cl 1 rests: since c371 the lower octave of T is Vln I (gli altri) + Vln II, so a ppp clarinet at pitch was a third same-octave layer that nobody hears (Adler II PDF 229, 217: at-pitch doublings swallow each other; a new colour per entrance), and its entry at 361 is now fresh (PDF 228)
% c381: 345-348 Cl 1 a sixth under Ob 1's T (bars 5-8 in A): the coda is the first time T is heard harmonised, as Rimsky-Korsakov sets a pastoral 3/4 tune for paired winds over harp (PDF 70: sixths are best on two different instruments in the normal order, Ob over Cl; Ex. 68 The Christmas Night) and as Dvorak's E-major trio turns its unison Fl+Ob tune into thirds when it returns (9/iii, Mutopia woods.notesS.ily, trio bars 17-24). Cl 1 still rests 349-360, so its 361 entry stays fresh (c379).
% c382: 345 Cl 1 A4 on beat 3 tied into 346 (was G sharp 4): the G sharp sounded against the A held in the harp (A4) and violas (A3), the 7th that is prepared here, held through ii7 and V7/V and resolves to G sharp only at 348 (Aldwell-Schachter PDF 177: the 7th of II enters as a held common tone and resolves down by step; PDF 175 Ex. 12-6a). Sixths with Ob 1 kept on every other beat.
codaClOne = { R2.*4 | g'2.\p\< | fis'2. | f'2 e'4 | dis'2. | e''4-.\ff b'8-. b'-. e''4-. | g''2->( fis''8 f''8 | e''4-.)\< b'8-. b'-. g''4-. | dis''2.--(\fff | dis''2.-- | dis''2.--) | e''4-> r4 r4 | R2. | R2.*4 | fis'2(\p^\markup \italic "dolce" a'4~ | a'4\< d''2-\tweak stencil ##f \mp\> | cis''4.-\tweak stencil ##f \p b'8 a'4 | fis'4 gis'2) | R2.*8 | R2.*4 | e''2.~\pp | e''2.~\> | e''2.~ | e''2 r4\! | R2.*11 | \once \override MultiMeasureRestText.parent-alignment-X = #RIGHT \once \override MultiMeasureRestText.self-alignment-X = #RIGHT \once \override MultiMeasureRestText.whiteout = #1 \once \override MultiMeasureRestText.layer = #3 R2.-\tag #'score ^\markup \small \italic \right-column { "muta in B♭" "(for the Finale)" } -\tag #'part _\markup \small \italic \right-column { "muta in B♭" "(for the Finale)" } | }
codaClTwo = { R2.*4 | e'2.\p\< | d'2. | a2 fis4 | b2. | b'2.\ff | b'2. | b'2.\< | a'2.--(\fff | a'2.-- | a'2.--) | g'4-> r4 r4 | R2. | R2.*20 | \partCombineChords b'2.~\pp | b'2.~\> | b'2.~ | b'2 r4\! | \partCombineAutomatic R2.*11 | \once \override MultiMeasureRestText.parent-alignment-X = #RIGHT \once \override MultiMeasureRestText.self-alignment-X = #RIGHT R2.-\tag #'part _\markup \small \italic \right-column { "muta in B♭" "(for the Finale)" } | }
codaBassCl = { R2.*4 | e,2.\p\< | d,2.\mp | R2. | R2. | R2.*8 |  % c016: B1 (written C sharp 3) is below the bass clarinet's low E flat (Adler PDF 216); the contrabassoon takes it
 R2.*36 | }
% c077: 345-348 Bsn 1 p dolce plays the trio's cello countermelody (tA 173-176) a fourth higher, under Ob 1's half of the tune (Adler PDF 267: a single bassoon line adds warmth; PDF 271: each wind's own colour makes a counter-line clear).
% c378: 345-347 Bsn 1's countermelody swells with Ob 1 (hidden mp at 346, back to p at 347): unshaped it was swallowed under the oboe's swell (Adler PDF 226; Tchaikovsky 6/i bassoon <> per group, PDF 227)
codaBsnOne = { R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | g2.\p\< | a2. | a2 e4 | a2. | g2.\ff | g2. | g2.\< | fis2.\fff | fis2-> fis4->~ | fis4 fis2-> | e4-> r4 r4 | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | \partCombineSoloI a4(\p\<^\markup \italic "dolce" b2~ | b4-\tweak stencil ##f \mp\> d2 | dis2-\tweak stencil ##f \p e4 | gis2 b4) \partCombineAutomatic | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | gis2.\pp | cis'2. | c'2. | b2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | }
codaBsnTwo = { R2.*4 | e2.\p\< | d2. | c2. | b,2. | e2.\ff | e2. | b,2.\< | b,2.\fff | b,2-> b,4->~ | b,4 b,2-> | e,4-> r4 r4 | R2. | R2.*16 | e2.\pp | e2. | e2. | e2. | R2.*16 | }
codaCbsn = { R2.*5 | d,2.\p\< | c,2. | b,,2.\f | e,,2.\ff | e,,2. | b,,2.\< | b,,2.\fff | b,,2-> b,,4->~ | b,,4 b,,2-> | e,,4-> r4 r4 | R2. | R2.*36 | }
% c029: 338 Hn 1 keeps B: the seventh A is Tpt 1's alone in the brass (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 102; as c019 at 104/300).
% c039: 331-332 the horns enter pp/p and crescendo into the ff at 333 (Hn 3-4 E-A on iv6 at 331, D sharp-B on V at 332; Hn 1-2 B-F sharp at 332), so the brass
%   join the coda's crescendo instead of entering at ff (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 127, 130; Ex. 258, PDF 421-422: horns, trumpets and tuba enter pp cresc. before the arrival). Low brass still enter at 333.
% c199: horns stay ff through 336-338 (no crescendo at 335, no fff re-attack at 336): Casanelles PDF 275 (loud brass: dissonant upper partials loudest), Dan's soft-brass rule; trumpets already f (c194)
% c225: "espress." at 357 stood level with the box W and touched it ("[W]espress."); horizontal padding stacks it under the letter (MOLA PDF 8, Ex. 7: anchored marks must not collide).
codaHnOne = { R2.*7 | b'2.--(\p\< | b'2.--\ff | b'2.-- | b'2.--) | b'2.--( | c''2.-- | b'2.--) | b'2--\> r4\! | R2. | R2.*16 | b'2.(\p-\tag #'score ^\markup \bold "1."\<-\tweak outside-staff-horizontal-padding #1 ^\markup \italic "espress." | ais'2.-\tweak stencil ##f \mp\> | a'2.-\sysLabel "1." | gis'2.)~ | gis'2.~\pp | gis'2.~ | gis'2.~ | gis'2.~ | gis'2.~ | gis'2 \tag #'score \once \override Rest.staff-position = #0 r4 | R2. | gis'2.~\pp | gis'2.~ | gis'2.~ | gis'2.~ | gis'2.~\> | gis'2.~ | gis'2.~ | gis'2.~\ppp | gis'2. | }
% c060: 357-360 Hn 2 rests (was E4 p under Hn 1's motto tail): the motto's chromatic tail is a single horn line, as movement I's horn call (I 13-14) and movement II's
%   clarinet tail (II 85-89) are single lines; E stays in the violas (357), Vln II (359-360), Bsn 2 and the basses. Sebesky PDF 12-13 (economy: Ex. 1 drops the line
%   under the lead in the lower-middle register), PDF 13-14 (focus). The shared Hn 1-2 staff had forced Hn 2 to p (c058). Hn 2 enters pp with the E-major chord at 361.
% c288: 361-362 Hn 2, Fl 1 and Cl 1-2 tied into 362, so the E-major chord of 361-364 is one attack in the winds as in the strings (Vln II, violas, cellos and basses tie from 361):
%   unmarked, the winds re-tongued it on 362's downbeat, where the diminuendo starts (Adler I PDF 309, 321). Hn 1's G sharp, the dynamics and the pitches are unchanged.
codaHnTwo = { R2.*7 | fis'2.--(\p\< | g'2.--\ff | g'2.-- | g'2.--) | fis'2.--( | fis'2.-- | fis'2.--) | e'2--\> r4\! | R2. | R2.*16 | \tag #'score \override MultiMeasureRest.transparent = ##t R2.*4 \tag #'score \revert MultiMeasureRest.transparent | e'2.~\pp | e'2.~ | e'2.~ | e'2 r4 | \tag #'score \override MultiMeasureRest.transparent = ##t R2.*2 \tag #'score \revert MultiMeasureRest.transparent R2.*10 | }
% c226: the trio tune's head at 341-344 moves from Hn 1 to Hn 3 (same notes). Hn 1 had 2.4 s between its ff at 339 and the p dolce entry, and then
%   only 345-356 to rest before the motto tail and the 12-s last G sharp; now it rests 340-356 (Adler PDF 292, 303: brass need frequent rests, the high horn most).
codaHnThree = { R2.*6 | f'2--(\pp\< e'4-- | dis'2.-- | e'2.--\ff | e'2.-- | e'2.--) | fis'2.--( | fis'2.-- | fis'2.--) | e'2--\> r4\! | R2. | \partCombineSoloI cis'2(\p^\markup \italic "dolce" d'4 | e'2 a'4 | gis'4. b8 d'4\<~ | d'4-\tweak stencil ##f \mp\> cis'2) | \partCombineAutomatic R2.\! | R2.*19 | gis'2.~\pp-\tag #'score ^\markup \bold "3." | gis'2.~ | gis'2.~ | gis'2.~ | gis'2 r4 | R2.*7 | }  % c325: "3." at 365 (Hn 3 re-enters after 20 bars' rest on the shared staff; the combiner's "3." was at 341; Adler II PDF 301 Ex. XVIII-10a, c135)
% c155: Hn 3·4 back to treble clef before the barline of their entry at 331, not right after their last note at 320 (moved from dC.ily).
% Gould, Behind Bars PDF 22: the clef goes before the barline of the entry bar, whether or not rests precede it; PDF 23: better than an
% immediate clef change before rests, which draws attention to the new clef at an arbitrary point.
codaHnFour = { R2.*6 | \tag #'score \clef treble a2--(\pp\< fis4-- | b2.-- | b2.--\ff | b2.-- | b2.--) | b2.--( | b2.-- | b2.--) | b2--\> r4\! | R2. | \partCombineSoloI R2.*4 | \partCombineAutomatic R2.*32 | }
% c188: 331/332-338 horns and 333-335 low brass slurred with tenuto (333-335 and 336-338 as two soft-tongued groups), so the fff arrival and the hemiola 337-338 are not covered by
%   unmarked re-tongued downbeats; the trumpets already have tenutos at 336-338 (c128). No accents, dynamics unchanged (Adler I PDF 309, 321; BRIEF: weight, not bite).
% c194: 333-334 and 339 Tbn 2 B2 (was E3, a third octave of the bass E1/E2 already in tuba and bass trombone): the trombones now hold the complete triad E2-B2-G3 over the tuba,
%   with Tbn 2 rising to E3 at 335 over the bass trombone's B2 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 101: the bass trombone and tuba form the bass in octaves, the other trombones take the upper parts;
%   PDF 100: brass harmony in close order with no empty spaces; PDF 114: balance matters most in sustained chords). Bn 2 E3 (333-334) and Bn 1 E3 (339) keep the octave above the bass filled (c114).
% c194: 335-338 Tpt 1-2 stay f (no crescendo; were cresc. to fff at 336): with two trumpets the four horns cannot go in pairs, so the horns are marked a degree louder than the
%   trumpets (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 103), as at 333 (horns ff, trumpets f, c086), and the wood-wind a degree louder than the brass (PDF 112). Horns, wood-wind, strings and timpani keep
%   the single fff at 336 (c028, c168). No jump f -> fff (c128's concern) and no accent; Dan's rule (BRIEF: brass weight, not bite).
codaTptOne = { R2.*8 | b'4-.\f b'8-. b'-. b'4-. | R2. | b'4-. b'8-. b'-. b'4-. | b'2.-- | a'2-- a'4--~ | a'4 a'2-- | g'2--\> r4\! | R2. | R2.*36 | }
codaTptTwo = { R2.*8 | e'4-.\f e'8-. e'-. e'4-. | R2. | e'4-. e'8-. e'-. e'4-. | dis'2.-- | dis'2-- dis'4--~ | dis'4 dis'2-- | e'2--\> r4\! | R2. | R2.*36 | }
% c086: 333-335 trombones, bass trombone and tuba f, a degree under the horns' ff, as in the tuttis at 105 and 305 (c076): a trombone at mf or louder has about twice a horn's power
%   (Sebesky PDF 32 note 1, PDF 46), and the lower parts should not exceed the upper ones in intensity (PDF 53); the trumpets' motive x is f above them. fff at 339 unchanged.
% c169: 339 trombones, bass trombone and tuba re-enter at f (were ff) after their rests at 336-338, with the diminuendo kept: Dan's rule (BRIEF, c123: no ff on brass entries;
%   weight by doubling, not by attack); the chord's weight is in Cbsn, Bsn 2, Vc and Cb (ff) and the horns and trumpets already sounding (Adler II PDF 147: the trombones marked
%   below the tutti so as not to dominate; PDF 28, Schubert Rosamunde: trombones bolster the low strings; PDF 164: a chord that must diminuendo goes to players who can control it).
% c204: 339 \partCombineChords on the trombone staff: after the shared rests 336-338 LilyPond's part combiner printed and played only Tbn 1's G3 and dropped Tbn 2's B2
%   (the parts had it), so the score's last tutti chord lost the trombones' fifth that c194 gave them (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 101; appendix of tutti chords PDF 505-507: trombones in close position over the tuba's bass).
% c393 (Rimsky-Korsakov ch. IV 'Economy in orchestral colour', PDF 136: after a long rest the re-entry of the horns, trombones and tuba should coincide
%   with pp or ff, 'piano and forte re-entries are less successful'; PDF 127: the second group enters piano or pianissimo and attains its crescendo more rapidly;
%   PDF 112: crescendo by adding strings, wood-wind, brass in that order): 332 trombones, bass trombone and tuba enter pp< on V (F sharp 3, B2 / B2 / B1), one bar
%   behind the horns (331-332, c039), after 211 bars' rest, and arrive at the f of 333 (c086) by crescendo instead of starting on it. Dan's rule kept: no ff, no accent.
codaTbnOne = { R2.*7 | fis2.--\pp\< | g2.--(\f | g2.-- | g2.--) | R2.*3 | \partCombineChords g2--\f\> r4\! | \partCombineAutomatic R2. | R2.*36 | }
codaTbnTwo = { R2.*7 | b,2.--\pp\< | b,2.--(\f | b,2.-- | e2.--) | R2.*3 | b,2--\f\> r4\! | R2. | R2.*36 | }
codaBassTbn = { R2.*7 | b,2.^-\pp\< | e,2.^-(\f | e,2.^- | b,2.^-) | R2.*3 | e,2--\f\> r4\! | R2. | R2.*36 | }
codaTuba = { R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | b,,2.^-\pp\< | e,,2.^-(\f | e,,2.^- | b,,2.^-) | R2.*3 | e,,2--\f\> r4\! | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | }
% c090: 371 motive x ppp (B-B-B-E) instead of the E roll at 372-374, so the timpani's last statements are the scherzo's own motive every four bars (363, 367, 371)
%   and the stroke at 375. The pulse-pulse-roll close under Vln I's held note was movement I's last scoring (I 179-182: pulse, pulse, ppp roll, harp chord, Vln I C5);
%   movement II removed its own roll and harp chord so as not to end in that scoring a second time (II c050, c120). Adler II PDF 263 (a colour kept fresh even after the
%   first movement has been heard), PDF 273 (Beethoven, Violin Concerto i: the timpani plays the motive between the phrases). Last sound unchanged: Hn 1 and Vln I G sharp.
% c236: "near the rim" for the ppp motive x and last stroke 371-375 (Adler II PDF 80: "a beautiful pianissimo ... closer to the rim"), as movement II marks its closing ppp roll; print only.
% c269: 332 the timpani roll after two bars' rest prints its starting level, mf< into ff at 333 (Adler II PDF 184, Bloch Schelomo: a roll in a build is marked at a level under the orchestra, then crescendo; as the other build rolls at 101, 205, 301).
% c326: the high E drum (23", E3), tuned since bar 1, played one note (11). At the coda's tonic arrival 333 motive x falls the octave E3-E3-E3-E2,
%   the shape of B-B-B-E, and the climax's last stroke 339 is the octave E2+E3 on the two drums at once: Beethoven's octave tuning in 9/ii (Adler II PDF 79),
%   two notes at once on a four-drum set (PDF 81, Ex. XII-50), the octave saved for the peak (PDF 126, 135: Brahms 2's restraint). Rhythm and levels unchanged.
% c294: 333 timpani f (was ff), as at 105 and 305 (c056), crescendo through 335 to a ff roll at 336 (was fff), a degree under the tutti each time:
%   Rimsky-Korsakov's tutti chords and examples mark the timpani at or under the level of the rest (appendix PDF 505-507, chords 1, 9, 20;
%   Ex. 204 PDF 367 f under ff, Ex. 206 PDF 369 ff under fff, Ex. 207 PDF 370 f under ff); with the low brass out at 336-338 (c034) a fff roll
%   with accents on B2 stood above the trumpets' f and the horns' ff. The fff peak stays in strings and woodwinds (c200, c258).
% c416: octave E at 339 damped (staccato), short like the strings and woodwinds, so it does not ring ff through the G.P. at 340 (Adler II PDF 80)
codaTimp = { R2.*4 | e,4\mf r4 r4 | R2. | R2. | b,2.:32\mf\< | e4--\f e8 e8 e,4 | R2. | b,4\< b,8 b,8 b,4 | b,2.:32\ff | b,2-> b,4->~ | b,4 b,2-> | <e, e>4-.-> r4 r4 | R2. | R2.^\markup \small \italic "soft sticks" | R2.*15 | R2.*6 | b,4\pp b,8 b,8 e,4 | R2. | R2.*2 | b,4 b,8 b,8 e,4 | R2. | R2.*2 | b,4\ppp^\markup \small \italic "near the rim" b,8 b,8 e,4 | R2.*3 | e,4\ppp r4 r4 | \once \override MultiMeasureRestText.parent-alignment-X = #RIGHT \once \override MultiMeasureRestText.self-alignment-X = #RIGHT R2.-\tag #'score ^\markup \small \italic \right-column { "muta: low E in G, G in A♭," "B in C, high E in E♭" "(for the Finale, in the pause)" } -\tag #'part _\markup \small \italic \right-column { "muta: low E in G, G in A♭," "B in C, high E in E♭" "(for the Finale, in the pause)" } | }  % c020: the retuning for the finale, marked at the end of this movement as movement II marks it for the scherzo (Adler II PDF 81; MOLA PDF 5)  % c235: muta text above the timpani staff, as the mutas at 145 and 225 (Adler II PDF 294: "written above the player's staff")
% c076: 333 the pedal setting for the coda (C sharp, D natural) printed in the rests, as at 73 and 141 (was printed under the chord at 341, the moment it is needed after 104 bars rest); "non arpegg." on the off-beat chords (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 46: harp chords are broken unless the composer says otherwise).
% c076: 375 the harmonic chord as two notes in the LH (E3, G sharp 3) and one in the RH (B3): Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 47, chords in harmonics consist of three close notes, two for the left hand and one for the right.
% c130: 375 the harmonics sound an octave above the written notes (Adler I PDF 116: 'notated with a zero over the note so that it will sound an octave higher';
%   'harp harmonics are very soft'). The MIDI played the written E3-G sharp 3-B3 at p, the loudest notes of the last chord, an octave under the sounding E4-G sharp 4-B4.
%   \harmOct (\transposition c'' for the one chord, then back to c'; and midiMaximumVolume 0.6) makes score.midi (and every render made from it) play E4-G sharp 4-B4; midiMaximumVolume 0.6 puts the p harmonics under
%   Hn 1's pp G sharp. MIDI only: the print is unchanged (closes c116's open point). Casanelles PDF 211-212: the mockup is how the score is heard and judged.
% c425: harp RH back to the treble clef at the system break 358|359, after its last bass-clef chord at 348 and before 12 bars' rest (Gould, Behind Bars PDF 23: after rests return to the commonest clef at the end of the system after the player has finished, not at an arbitrary point before the entry; the system at 359 now opens in the harp's usual clef).
% c099: 345-348 the harp's chords up an octave (D4-B4, between Bsn 1's dolce countermelody D3-B3 and Ob 1's tune D5-B5): in the bass clef they sat in the bassoon's band and masked its line (Casanelles PDF 277-278: instruments in one frequency band mask each other; PDF 274: low register wants wide spacing). The \clef treble moves from 349 to 345.
harmOct = #(define-music-function (m) (ly:music?) #{ \set PianoStaff.midiMaximumVolume = #0.6 \transposition c'' #m \transposition c' #})
% c357: 343 harp beat 2 repeats the beat-3 chord <b, e gis>: the old <d e gis> (D3-E3) was the only harp second below G3, rubbed the violas' held E3 and doubled Hn 3's seventh D under the solo horn (Sebesky PDF 12-13, Ex. 1: lighten the lower-middle register so the focus comes through).
codaHarpR = { R2.*16 | \clef bass r4 <cis e a>4^\markup \small \italic "non arpegg." <cis e a>4 | r4 <cis e a>4 <cis e a>4 | r4 <b, e gis>4 <b, e gis>4 | r4 <cis e a>4 <cis e a>4 | r4 <fis a d'>4 <fis a d'>4 | r4 <fis a d'>4 <fis a d'>4 | r4 <fis a b>4 <fis a b>4 | r4 <gis b e'>4 <gis b e'>4 | R2.*3 R2.*7 \clef treble R2.*2 | <e' gis' b' e''>2.\arpeggio | R2.*13 | \harmOct { b4-\flageolet } r4 r4 | R2. | }  % c026: 375 the last chord as harmonics in the LH (below)  % c390: 345-348 off-beats F sharp 3-E4, under Cl 1's lowest note F sharp 4 (were D4-B4, striking Cl 1's F sharp 4, A4, B4 and G sharp 4 since c381 put Cl 1 a sixth under Ob 1): keep the accompaniment registrally distant from a woodwind solo (Adler II PDF 274), as the trio's own off-beats lie under Cl 1's T at 153 (G sharp 3-E4)
codaHarpL = { R2.*8 | \once \override MultiMeasureRestText.self-alignment-X = #LEFT \once \override MultiMeasureRestText.parent-alignment-X = #LEFT R2._\markup \small "Ped.: D♮ C♯ B♮ / E♮ F♯ G♯ A♮" | R2.*7 | a,,4\pp r4 r4 | a,,4 r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | a,,4 r4 r4 | d,4 r4 r4 | b,,4 r4 r4 | b,,4 r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | R2.*12 | <e, b,>2.\arpeggio\pp | R2.*13 | \harmOct { <e gis>4-\flageolet\p^\markup \small \italic "harm." } r4 r4 | R2. | }  % c026: 375 harmonics E3-G#3-B3 (sounding E4-G#4-B4); c076: E3 and G#3 in the LH, B3 in the RH (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 47); the low E1/E2 strings no longer ring past the last G#
% c033: 329-332 Vln I rises G5-A5-C6-D#6 against the falling bass E-D-C-B (a diverging progression: Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 131), so the crescendo widens the
%   space that the woodwinds and brass fill at 333 and D#6 resolves by step into the ff E6; Cl 1 keeps c032's tenths with the bass (G4-F#4-E4-D#4).
% c089: 331 Vln I F6-C6-C6-F6 (was C6-A5-A5-C6): C6 at 331 went to D sharp 6 at 332, the score's only melodic augmented 2nd (Aldwell-Schachter PDF 89: 'the melodic
%   augmented 2nd is forbidden'; c022 removed the others). Now flat 2 in the top voice falls a diminished 3rd to the leading tone, F6-D sharp 6-E6 (A-S PDF 483-484, Ex. 28-2:
%   flat 2 in the soprano is the most usual and most intense position; the diminished 3rd to sharp 7 is 'peculiarly intense'). Outer voices 331-332: 11th -> 10th over C -> B; no octaves.
% c134: 353 and 355 hidden pp closing the swell 349-352 and c098's hairpin at 355 (MIDI only, prints nothing): with a bare \! the decrescendo ran in the MIDI to the next printed dynamic (ppp at 369),
%   so the render played the top of T's last cadence 353-356 at velocity 31-35, under Vln II (45), the violas (37) and the basses (50) (Adler II PDF 159, 164).
% c258: 349 "dolce" on Vln I and Vln II, as on Cl 1, which doubles their T an octave lower at the same pp (Sebesky Ex. 281, PDF 214-215; Ex. 2A, PDF 18: each entry's character word).
codaVlnOne = { R2.*3 | b''4-.\pp\< b''8-. b''-. b''4-. | g''4-.\mf\< e''8-. e''-. g''4-. | a''4-. fis''8-. fis''-. a''4-. | f'''4-. c'''8-. c'''-. e'''4-. | dis'''4-.\f b''8-. b''-. r4 | e'''4-.\ff b''8-. b''-. e'''4-. | g'''2->\dnb( fis'''8 f'''8 | e'''4-.)\< fis'''8-. g'''-. a'''4-. | b'''2.->\fff\dnb | a'''2->\dnb fis'''4->~ | fis'''4 dis'''2-> | e'''4->\dnb r4 r4 | R2. | R2.*8 | <cis'' cis'''>2(\pp\<\upbow^\markup \italic \column { "dolce" "div.: 2 soli / gli altri" } <dis'' dis'''>4 | <e'' e'''>4 <gis'' gis'''>4) <a'' a'''>4~-\tweak stencil ##f \p( | <a'' a'''>4.\> <gis'' gis'''>8 <fis'' fis'''>4) | <cis'' cis'''>4( <b' b''> <a' a''>) | <gis' gis''>4-\tweak stencil ##f \pp( <b' b''> <e'' e'''> | <gis'' gis'''>2-- <fis'' fis'''>8 <e'' e'''>~ | <e'' e'''>4\> <dis'' dis'''>-\tweak stencil ##f \pp <fis'' fis'''>) | <e'' e'''>2 r4 | R2.*12 | gis''2.~\ppp^\markup \italic "tutti, change bow freely" | gis''2.~ | gis''2.~ | gis''2.~ | gis''2.~ | gis''2.~ | gis''2.~ | gis''2. | }  % c371: 349-356 div. in octaves, the upper octave (C#6-A6) for 2 solo violins, the rest of Vln I an octave lower with Vln II and Cl 1; tutti again at 369 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 59 note to Ex. 24, PDF 60 Ex. 26: pp cantabile in two octaves, two solo violins on top, all the other 1st violins in the lower octave). c016: 340 beat 3 rest (was A4): time for the shift to E6 at 341 (Adler PDF 82-83); f moved to the downbeat of 340
% c024: 339 the strings play the closing ff chord in multiple stops (Vln II G3-E4-B4-G5, Vla B3-G4-E5, Vc E2-B2) instead of unison E's, as in Rimsky-Korsakov's single tutti chords (PDF 505-507)
codaVlnTwo = { R2.*2 | e''4-.\pp e''8-. e''-. e''4-. | g''2->(\< fis''8 f''8 | e''4-.)\mf\< b'8-. b'-. e''4-. | e''4-. b'8-. b'-. e''4-. | f''4-. a'8-. a'-. ais'4-. | b'4-. fis'8-. fis'-. b'4-. | e''4-.\ff b'8-. b'-. e''4-. | g''2->( fis''8 f''8 | e''4-.)\< fis''8-. g''-. a''4-. | b''2.->\fff | a''2->\dnb fis''4->~ | fis''4 dis''2-> | <g e' b' g''>4->\dnb^\markup \italic "non div." r4 r4 | R2. | R2.*8 | cis''2(\pp\<\upbow^\markup \italic "dolce" dis''4 | e''4 gis''4) a''4~-\tweak stencil ##f \p( | a''4.\> gis''8 fis''4) | cis''4( b'4 a'4) | gis'4-\tweak stencil ##f \pp( b'4 e''4 | gis''2-- fis''8 e''8~ | e''4\> dis''4-\tweak stencil ##f \pp fis''4) | e''2 r4 | gis'2.\pp | g'2. | e'2. | e'2. | gis'2.~ | gis'2.~\> | gis'2.~ | gis'2 r4\! | R2.*12 | }  % c011: 357-364 Vln II doubles T an octave under Vln I (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 59, 62); its held notes go to the violas  % c123: 362-364 strings and woodwinds fade into Hn 1's G sharp instead of stopping together at pp (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 127, 130: diminuendo by elimination)
% c071: 336-338 violas play the peak line B4-A4-F sharp 4-D sharp 4, a third octave under Vln I/Fl 1 (B6) and Vln II/Ob 1 (B5), instead of a fifth held D sharp 4
%   (Adler II PDF 24, Elgar 'W.M.B.': octave and double-octave doublings for the massive sound; PDF 20, Bruckner 7/iv: the climax built in octaves).
% c327: 343 violas G sharp 3 (was B3): in the coda recall Hn 3's new bar 3 (G sharp 4-B3-E4 in A, c321) fell onto the violas' held B3, the only sustained accompaniment, so the solo's
%   falling sixth disappeared into it (Adler II PDF 264: the strings in subsidiary roles under a solo horn; PDF 273-274). G sharp 3 is a lower neighbour A-G sharp-A that resolves the leading tone with the harp.
% c347: 343 violas E3 (was G sharp 3): A3-G sharp 3 under Hn 3's A4-G sharp 4 (342 beat 3 -> 343) moved in octaves with the solo and doubled its leading tone in the only
%   sustained string part (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 112: correct progression of parts first; PDF 113: strings and brass are seldom laid together, so a held string part should not shadow the horn's line).
%   E3 is the root of the V7 with the harp's E3; c327's aim (no held B3 under the solo's falling sixth) is kept. G sharp stays in the harp (G sharp 3 -> A3 at 344).
% c362 (A&S Unit 5, PDF 76): G#3 added at 353 so the cadential 6/4 keeps its sixth on beats 2-3 (div. from 353)
codaVla = { R2. | b4-.\pp b8-. b-. b4-. | d'2->( cis'8 c'8 | b4-.)\< b8-. b-. b4-. | b4-.\mf\< fis8-. fis-. b4-. | fis4-. d8-. d-. fis4-. | a4-. c'8-. c'-. fis4-. | b4-. fis8-. fis-. b4-. | e'4-.\ff b8-. b-. e'4-. | g'2->( fis'8 f'8 | e'4-.)\< fis'8-. g'-. a'4-. | b'2.->\fff | a'2->\dnb fis'4->~ | fis'4 dis'2-> | <b g' e''>4->\dnb^\markup \italic "non div." r4 r4 | R2. | a2.\pp | a2. | e2. | a2. | a2.\ppp | d'2. | b2. | b2. | <e' gis'>2.\ppp^\markup \italic "div." | <e' b'>2. | <e' a'>2. | e'2.^\markup \italic "unis." | <gis e'>2.~^\markup \italic "div." | <gis e'>2. | <a fis'>2. | <gis e'>2. | e'2.\pp^\markup \italic "unis." | cis'2. | c'2. | b2. | b2.~ | b2.~\> | b2.~ | b2 r4\! | R2.*12 | }  % c011: div. 357-364 (Vln II's held notes added)
% c107: 349-356 the cellos take up the trio's countermelody (tC 217-220, the same harmony C sharp m - E/G sharp - F sharp m - F sharp m/A) where Bsn 1's countermelody ends (348),
%   so the last statement of T keeps its middleground (Adler I PDF 132, 142-143: all three layers, the middleground heard earlier; PDF 154, 163: a line handed on and dovetailed);
%   353-356 arco on the bass notes they plucked (B2, E3), so the cadence voicing 354-355 is unchanged; Cb stays pizz. 348 rest: five beats from pizz to arco (c085).
% c148: 341 Vc and Cb pizz. pp (were p) under Hn 1's p dolce and the harp's pp, as at 153 (c099); they stay pp into 349.
% c267: 341-347 cellos rest (were pizz. A2-A2-E3-A2-D3-B2-B2 at the octave above the basses' pizz. roots, with the harp LH on the same roots): the soft first half of T (Hn 3, then Ob 1 with Bsn 1) now has the trio's own bass, harp and pizz. basses only, as at 153-168 and 185-188 (c148 asked for 'as at 153', where no cello plays). The cellos then enter fresh at 349 with the countermelody (c107), already arco from 339, so no 'arco' is printed. Sebesky PDF 140, 145-146 (rule 7: the root is the least important note; doubling the bass with the cellos out of habit grounds the sound; the bottom cello takes roots only for temporary richness), PDF 150-151 (Ex. 201-202: the low strings enter as a unison counter line under the high violins), PDF 168 (Ex. 214: lead horn over a light accompaniment, the bass to pizzicato).
% c362 (A&S Unit 5, PDF 83): 331 motive x on A2-F2 (was F3-A2), no octaves A-F with Vln I's A5-F6 into the Neapolitan 6/4
% c420: 352 cellos hold F sharp 3 through the bar (was F sharp 3 - C sharp 3), so the bar is ii6/5 throughout (bass A, F sharp, E, melody C sharp-B-A), as at 220 (cellos fis2.) and 164 (harp A-C sharp-F sharp); beat 3 no longer sounds a plain A-major triad under the melody's A right after the eight A-major bars 341-348 (A&S PDF 451: emphasis on IV makes IV heard as I and I as V; PDF 443, 456 point 2: the new key confirmed by a cadence with II as well as V). F sharp 3 -> B2 into the 6/4 as at 220-221.
