% c048: 337-338 the hemiola cadence at the coda's peak is played by the bass and the middle as well as the tune (Aldwell-Schachter PDF 51-52, Ex. 3-8:
%   Mozart K. 283 puts the bass in the hemiola too): Vc, Cb, Cbsn, Bsn 1-2, violas, Tpt 1-2 (tutti rhythm) and timpani strike the three minims
%   (beat 1 and 3 of 337, beat 2 of 338) instead of restriking on 338's downbeat; the tune's syncopated minims carry accents (A-S PDF 51, Ex. 3-6 sf).
%   Horns and Fl 2/Ob 2/Cl 1-2 keep the bar's dotted minims, as the horns do against the second idea at 45. Pitches unchanged.
% c048: 353 tenuto on the cadential 6/4's G sharp (Vln I, Vln II, Cl 1): the accented dissonance of the last cadence (A-S PDF 54-55, Ex. 3-15).
% c074: 331 Cl 2 A3 (was C4): on the iv6 the bass C is doubled near the bottom (Cb, Cbn C2, Bsn 2 C3) and in Vln I's line, not also in the middle at C4,
%   so the 6/3 keeps its open sound and the root A is in the woodwind chord (Adler I PDF 152, Ex. V-27).
% c083: 330 Vc A2-F sharp 2, violas F sharp 3-D3 (were E3-B2 and B3-F sharp 3): the natural VII (D major) under the falling bass E-D-C-B is a whole chord in the low strings, so I - natural VII - Neapolitan 6/4 - V7 is heard as A-S Ex. 15-15/15-16 (PDF 238: natural VII through a passing 6/3 to V7, 4 = A in Bsn 1 and Vln I prepares V7's seventh); Vln II keeps E5-B4 above. No parallels (Vla B3-F sharp 3 avoids 12ths with the bass E-D).
% c080: bar 331 iv6 -> Neapolitan 6/4 (C-F-A): E->F in Vln II, Vc, Cl 1, Hn 3; violas C4 quavers (bass doubled); F falls to D#4 in Cl 1 and Hn 3 (A&S PDF 483, 490-491, Ex. 28-15)
% c332: 331 beat 3 French sixth C-E-F#-A# after the Neapolitan 6/4 on the same bass C (A-S PDF 525 sec. 25 and review 9, PDF 527: the 'Neapolitan 6/4' decorates the augmented sixth
%   like a cadential 6/4; the German form C-E-G-A# is excluded by the C-major rule, so the French). Vln I F6-E6-D#6 and Cl 1/Hn 3 F4-E4-D#4 fill flat 2's diminished 3rd by 1 (PDF 483-484, Ex. 28-2c/e);
%   Vln II A4-A#4-B4 is 4-#4-5 over the bass C-B (Ex. 29-3a), the augmented sixth Vc C3/Vln II A#4 opening to the octave B2-B4 (PDF 503, Ex. 29-2); Vla, Cl 2, Hn 4 F#3 (2, the French sixth's
%   common tone with V, PDF 507); Bsn 1 E3. The bass E-D-C-B is A-S's stepwise descent from I to the augmented sixth on flat 6 (PDF 510-511, Ex. 29-17). F stays on beats 1-2 (finale IV 272).
% c081: T bars 10-11 (162, 178, 218, 350): the climax A is anticipated on beat 3 of bar 10 and tied over the bar line (was e4 gis2 | a4. gis8 fis4, now e4 gis4 a4~ | a4. gis8 fis4).
%   The phrase's highest note becomes its longest and a syncopation, the only bar-line tie before the cadence (Aldwell-Schachter Unit 3 PDF 50: long and high notes take accent;
%   PDF 51: a note begun on a weak beat and held through the strong beat contradicts the metre; PDF 56, Ex. 3-19: the anticipation held over into the consonance, here I6 -> ii).
%   Dvorak 9/iii's E-major tune (Ob 1, 68-76) ties over five bar lines in nine bars. Theme change (third phrase only); violins re-bow on the A.
% c112: 346 harp ii7 B-D-F#-A (A common tone held 345-347 as the 7th; A-S PDF 177). 351-352 violas E4 held: ii7-ii6/5 into the 6/4 at 353 (A-S Ex. 12-8, PDF 181-182).
% c174: 349 violas ppp under the pp tune, as at 345 (Casanelles PDF 271-272: mark the background for the result heard)
% c295: 361-364 Cl 2's B3 was not printed: the part combiner dropped it after the shared rest (probe of noteheads against the uncombined parts); \partCombineChords prints <b e'> on one stem (Gould, Behind Bars PDF 41: shared ledger line). 366 Hn 1's rest centred: Hn 2's rests are hidden at 365-366, so the bar reads as one part (Gould PDF 49: rests stay centred in a single part).
% c377: 361-364 Cl 1-2 hold E5/B4 (were E4/B3): the clarinets sat in unison with Hn 2's E4 and the violas' B3, so every note of the fading E-major chord was a wind-string unison
%   and nothing sustained between G sharp 4 and Fl 1's G sharp 5 once the harp's B4/E5 (361) died. Adler I PDF 257: overlapping at the unison robs both timbres and only gives a
%   stronger pitch, and the upper notes of a chord are voiced close with the wide gaps at the bottom (Ex. VIII-24); PDF 252 (Brahms 2/iii, Ex. VIII-18): the last chord is coloured
%   by a wind chord in its own voicing beside the strings'. The chord now reads E2 E3 B3 E4 G#4 (strings, horns) | B4 E5 G#5 (clarinets, flute), the harp's own spacing at 361;
%   Fl 1 over Cl at pp is Adler's Ex. VIII-22 'better for p or pp'. Hn 1's G sharp, the hairpins, ties and the fade into 365 are unchanged.

% s395 (cycle 395): Hn 1 "1." at 359, now the first bar of a system (the solo runs on from 357; c235 restates the player at a system start, Adler II PDF 295)
